   This document includes some information useful for installing and
using a prototype robust parser named `R-LRPar'.

1) General Information

 -  The software is useful for research on natural language 
    processing.

 -  The software is constructed for studying syntactic analysis of
    ill-formed sentences. It is promoted and granted by AITEC(*) under 
    the project named IFS(**).

    (*) Research Institute for Advanced Information Technology
    (*) ICOT Free Software

 -  The whole software is stored in the directory named "src". 
    The component of this software is described in section 3.

 -  There is also a manual written in Japanese. It is kept in a file
    named "Manual" (EUC Code) and "Manual.jis" (JIS Code)

2) What is R-LRPar ? 

    R-LRPar is an extended GLR parser for analyzing ill-formed sentences.
As the GLR method is well-known as a fastest parsing algorithm,
R-LRPar is expected to inherit this property and then it will parse
ill-formed sentences speedily. 
    In general, most of grammatical ill-formedness (later also, called
errors) can be classified into four types: (1) Extra Constituents (2)
Missing Constituents (3) Substituted Constituents.  The detail of
types of ill-formedness is described in [1][4]. 
    The parser can detect and correct multiple arbitrary errors in the
analyzed sentence.  However, without any constraint, it takes a large
amount of computation time to parse a relatively long ill-formed
sentence because the parser will try to find all parsing possibilities
through very large search space. To solve this problem, a reasonable
heuristic is applied to reduce the search space during an ill-formed
sentence is analyzed. 
    R-LRPar can certainly parse any grammatical sentence. In this
case, the parsing speed of the grammatical sentence is not sacrificed
even if the original GLR parsing algorithm is modified by adding some
mechanisms to deal ill-formed sentences.

3) Software Characteristics

    - The software parse an input sentence under constraints given
      in the form of a context free grammar. As the result, It outputs
      the syntactic trees which are the interpretation of the sentence. 
      The interpretation result can be applied later in the succeeding
      processing, such as semantic analysis module, discourse analysis
      module or any inference module. The algorithm applied is based
      on the fastest parsing algorithm named `Generalized LR parsing
      method'. Due to this, our software is expected to parse an input
      efficiently and with high speed.

    - This software can analyze not only grammatical inputs but
      also ungrammatically ill-formed inputs. Although it can robustly
      parse an _ill-formed_ input, efficiency of the analysis of
      _grammatical_ inputs is not sacrificed because the extra
      processing steps which are needed for analyzing ill-formed
      inputs are not executed.

    - This software includes a tagger which is used as a preprocessor
      for assigning the most appropriate lexical category to each word
      in an input before parsing. This tagger is based on the
      rule-based tagging method constructed by Brill. Before using it,
      please see its license.

4) Languages and Environments

    The software is written using Prolog, Perl, Shell, C languages.
    The main part is written in Prolog. The following shows the detail.

    -  Prolog  :  The main program of R-LRPar 
    -  Shell   :  The processing module before tagging each word in 
                  the analyzed sentence.
    -  Perl    :  The processing module after tagging each word in 
                  the analyzed sentence.
    -  C       :  The Tagger for assigning the most proper lexical 
                  category to each word in the sentence.
                  (Mostly, Brill's tagger)

    The desirable environment is as follows.

    -  The software should be executed in UNIX environment.

    -  The software requires sicstus prolog or any general-purpose prolog
       interpreter. Sicstus prolog is licensed by Swedish Institute of
       Computer Science.

    -  Perl interpreter is also needed in the system. You can find it 
       via the following URL. It is a free software

             http://www.perl.com/perl/

    -  Some parts of this software are constructed by using shell
       script language. That is an interpreter of shell script is 
       needed.

    -  C compiler is also required if you want to modified the original
       Brill's tagger.
 
